# Template Method

Write the fixed sequence of steps once, in a base class, and let each case fill in only the steps that differ.

## Use it when

- several cases follow the same sequence of steps, and only some steps change from case to case;
- a change to the sequence itself (a new first step, a new check at the end) should apply to every case at once;
- new cases following the same sequence are expected.

## Do not use it when

- there is no shared sequence: each case is a different algorithm (that is Strategy);
- there are two cases and the shared part is a couple of lines;
- the only shared thing is a formatting helper: a utility function is enough, and it is not a Template Method.

## Shape

```java
abstract class BoardGame {
    // the template method: the sequence lives here and nowhere else
    public final Result play(List<Player> players) {
        Board board = setUp(players);
        while (!isOver(board)) {
            playTurn(board, nextPlayer(board, players));
        }
        return decideWinner(board);
    }

    protected abstract Board setUp(List<Player> players);
    protected abstract boolean isOver(Board board);
    protected abstract void playTurn(Board board, Player player);
    protected abstract Result decideWinner(Board board);

    protected Player nextPlayer(Board board, List<Player> players) { /* default rotation; a hook */ }
}

final class Checkers extends BoardGame { /* only the four steps */ }
final class Reversi extends BoardGame { /* only the four steps */ }
```

The same idea works with composition: one generic runner that receives the varying steps as objects or functions
(`new GameRunner(setUp, isOver, playTurn, decideWinner)`), as long as every case goes through that runner.

## Done right when

- the sequence is written once, in the template method (marked `final` when the language allows);
- each case implements only the steps that vary, and none of them repeats the sequence;
- every case uses the skeleton, including the cases that existed before it was introduced;
- a change to the sequence is one edit, in the template method.

## Usual mistakes

- A common interface with one method (`run()`), where each implementation still writes the whole sequence: the
  cases look uniform from outside, but the skeleton is copied inside each one.
- A shared helper that formats the output, while each case still calls the steps in order by itself.
- The new cases use the skeleton and the old ones stay as they were.
- Steps so coarse that one "step" contains almost the whole sequence.

## Neighbors

- **Strategy**: the whole rule varies, there is no common skeleton.
- **Factory Method**: often appears as one of the steps of a template method ("create the piece").
# Factory Method

Put the decision of which concrete class to create in one place, so every caller that needs the object asks that
place instead of choosing with its own `if`.

## Use it when

- several concrete types share an interface, and the right one depends on some input (a kind, a level, a format);
- more than one place in the code needs to create them, and each would otherwise repeat the choice;
- new types are expected.

## Do not use it when

- every caller knows exactly which class it needs: call the constructor;
- there is only one concrete type;
- the object is complex to assemble but the type is always the same: that is Builder.

## Shape

Three accepted forms; pick the one that fits the code.

```java
// 1. Classic: a creator whose subclasses override the creation step
abstract class Level {
    abstract Enemy spawnEnemy(Position where);          // the factory method
    void populate(List<Position> spots) { spots.forEach(p -> add(spawnEnemy(p))); }
}
final class ForestLevel extends Level { Enemy spawnEnemy(Position p) { return new Wolf(p); } }
final class CaveLevel extends Level { Enemy spawnEnemy(Position p) { return new Bat(p); } }

// 2. A concentrated factory: one class or method that maps the input to the type
final class Enemies {
    static Enemy spawn(Terrain terrain, Position p) {
        return switch (terrain) {                        // the only place that chooses
            case FOREST -> new Wolf(p);
            case CAVE -> new Bat(p);
            case SWAMP -> new Slime(p);
        };
    }
}

// 3. Polymorphic creation on the input itself
sealed interface Terrain permits Forest, Cave, Swamp { Enemy spawnEnemy(Position p); }
```

## Done right when

- the choice of the concrete type is made in exactly one place;
- every place that needs the object asks that place, including the places that existed before the new types;
- no caller builds one of the types by hand "just this once";
- adding a type means one new class and one entry in the factory.

## Usual mistakes

- The same `if`/`switch` on the input appears in each place that creates the object, even if each branch calls a
  helper: the choice is still repeated.
- A factory exists, but one caller still creates the object directly.
- The factory always returns the same type, and the real choice happens afterwards, outside it.

## Neighbors

- **Abstract Factory**: several related products that must vary together.
- **Builder**: how to assemble one complex object, not which type to create.
- **Strategy**: the created objects are often strategies; the factory is just where they are chosen.

